{"track":"calculus","num":"4.01","slug":"antiderivatives-and-indefinite-integrals","title":"Antiderivatives and Indefinite Integrals","sections":[{"heading":"Section 1: The Power Rule for Integration","questions":[{"q":"Evaluate $\\int (6x^3 - 4x + 5)\\,dx$.","choices":["$\\frac{3}{2}x^4 - 2x^2 + 5x + C$","$2x^4 - 2x^2 + 5x + C$","$18x^2 - 4 + C$","$\\frac{3}{2}x^4 - 4x^2 + 5x + C$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\int 6x^3\\,dx = 6(x^4/4) = \\frac{3}{2}x^4$. $\\int -4x\\,dx = -2x^2$. $\\int 5\\,dx = 5x$. Result: $\\frac{3}{2}x^4 - 2x^2 + 5x + C$."},{"q":"Find $\\int \\left(x^{2/3} + \\frac{1}{x^3}\\right)\\,dx$.","choices":["$\\frac{3}{5}x^{5/3} - \\frac{1}{2x^2} + C$","$\\frac{5}{3}x^{5/3} - \\frac{1}{4x^4} + C$","$\\frac{3}{5}x^{5/3} + \\frac{1}{2x^2} + C$","$\\frac{2}{5}x^{5/3} - \\frac{2}{x^2} + C$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\int x^{2/3}\\,dx = \\frac{x^{5/3}}{5/3} = \\frac{3}{5}x^{5/3}$. $\\int x^{-3}\\,dx = \\frac{x^{-2}}{-2} = -\\frac{1}{2x^2}$. Result: $\\frac{3}{5}x^{5/3} - \\frac{1}{2x^2} + C$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\int \\frac{3x^4 - 2x}{x^2}\\,dx$.","choices":["$\\frac{3}{3}x^3 - 2\\ln|x| + C = x^3 - 2\\ln|x| + C$","$x^3 - \\frac{2}{x} + C$","$3x^3 - 2x + C$","$x^3 - 2x^{-1} + C$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Simplify integrand: $\\frac{3x^4}{x^2} - \\frac{2x}{x^2} = 3x^2 - \\frac{2}{x}$. Integrating: $x^3 - 2\\ln|x| + C$."}]},{"heading":"Section 2: Trigonometric and Exponential Antiderivatives","questions":[{"q":"Evaluate $\\int (3\\sin x + 4\\cos x)\\,dx$.","choices":["$-3\\cos x + 4\\sin x + C$","$3\\cos x - 4\\sin x + C$","$3\\cos x + 4\\sin x + C$","$-3\\cos x - 4\\sin x + C$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\int \\sin x\\,dx = -\\cos x$ and $\\int \\cos x\\,dx = \\sin x$. Thus $\\int (3\\sin x + 4\\cos x)\\,dx = -3\\cos x + 4\\sin x + C$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\int (5e^x + \\sec^2 x)\\,dx$.","choices":["$5e^x + \\tan x + C$","$5e^x - \\tan x + C$","$5e^x + \\sec x\\tan x + C$","$e^x + \\tan x + C$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\int e^x\\,dx = e^x$ and $\\int \\sec^2 x\\,dx = \\tan x$. Result is $5e^x + \\tan x + C$."},{"q":"Find $\\int (2^x + \\csc x\\cot x)\\,dx$.","choices":["$\\frac{2^x}{\\ln 2} - \\csc x + C$","$2^x\\ln 2 - \\csc x + C$","$\\frac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + \\csc x + C$","$2^x - \\cot x + C$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\int a^x\\,dx = \\frac{a^x}{\\ln a}$ and $\\int \\csc x\\cot x\\,dx = -\\csc x$. Result is $\\frac{2^x}{\\ln 2} - \\csc x + C$."}]},{"heading":"Section 3: Initial Value Problems","questions":[{"q":"Find $f(x)$ given $f'(x) = 4x - 3$ and $f(1) = 6$.","choices":["$f(x) = 2x^2 - 3x + 7$","$f(x) = 2x^2 - 3x + 5$","$f(x) = 4x^2 - 3x + 5$","$f(x) = 2x^2 - 3x$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f(x) = \\int (4x - 3)\\,dx = 2x^2 - 3x + C$. $f(1) = 2(1)^2 - 3(1) + C = 6 \\Rightarrow -1 + C = 6 \\Rightarrow C = 7$."},{"q":"A curve satisfies $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^2 + 2x$ and passes through $(2, 10)$. Find its equation.","choices":["$y = x^3 + x^2 - 2$","$y = x^3 + x^2 + 2$","$y = 3x^3 + 2x^2 - 22$","$y = x^3 + x^2$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$y = x^3 + x^2 + C$. $y(2) = 8 + 4 + C = 10 \\Rightarrow 12 + C = 10 \\Rightarrow C = -2$. Equation: $y = x^3 + x^2 - 2$."},{"q":"Given $f''(x) = 6x, f'(0) = 4$, and $f(0) = 1$, find $f(x)$.","choices":["$f(x) = x^3 + 4x + 1$","$f(x) = 3x^3 + 4x + 1$","$f(x) = x^3 + 2x + 1$","$f(x) = x^3 + 4$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f'(x) = 3x^2 + C_1$. $f'(0) = 4 \\Rightarrow C_1 = 4$. $f(x) = x^3 + 4x + C_2$. $f(0) = 1 \\Rightarrow C_2 = 1$."}]},{"heading":"Section 4: Rectilinear Motion and Acceleration","questions":[{"q":"A particle moves with acceleration $a(t) = 6t - 4$. If its initial velocity $v(0) = 5$, find $v(t)$.","choices":["$v(t) = 3t^2 - 4t + 5$","$v(t) = 6t^2 - 4t + 5$","$v(t) = 3t^2 - 4t$","$v(t) = 3t^2 + 4t + 5$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$v(t) = \\int (6t - 4)\\,dx = 3t^2 - 4t + C$. $v(0) = 5 \\Rightarrow C = 5$. Thus $v(t) = 3t^2 - 4t + 5$."},{"q":"If a ball is thrown upward with $v(0) = 48\\text{ ft/s}$ and $a(t) = -32\\text{ ft/s}^2$ from ground level $s(0) = 0$, find its maximum height.","choices":["$36\\text{ ft}$","$48\\text{ ft}$","$64\\text{ ft}$","$32\\text{ ft}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$v(t) = -32t + 48 = 0 \\Rightarrow t = 1.5\\text{ s}$. $s(t) = -16t^2 + 48t$. At $t = 1.5$: $s(1.5) = -16(2.25) + 48(1.5) = -36 + 72 = 36\\text{ ft}$."},{"q":"A car decelerates at a constant $a = -4\\text{ m/s}^2$ from $v_0 = 24\\text{ m/s}$. How far does it travel before coming to a stop?","choices":["$72\\text{ m}$","$48\\text{ m}$","$96\\text{ m}$","$36\\text{ m}$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$v(t) = 24 - 4t = 0 \\Rightarrow t = 6\\text{ s}$. Distance $s(t) = 24t - 2t^2$. At $t = 6$: $s(6) = 24(6) - 2(36) = 144 - 72 = 72\\text{ m}$."}]}]}